# SECTION 3: ARCHITECTURE PATTERNS & DESIGN DECISIONS
# ============================================================================
#
# === Observer Pattern ===
# Event-driven communication between components
#
#   * Used in: EventBus, NotificationManager, PubSubBroker
#   * Publishers emit events without knowledge of subscribers
#   * Subscribers register interest in specific event types
#   * Supports both synchronous and asynchronous delivery
#   * Event ordering guarantees within a single publisher
#   * Dead letter queue for failed deliveries
#   * Backpressure handling with configurable buffer sizes
#   * Event deduplication with idempotency keys
#   * Wildcard subscriptions for pattern-based filtering
#   * Event replay from persistent event log
#   * Circuit breaker on subscriber failures
#   * Metrics: events/sec, delivery latency, failure rate
#
# Implementation Guidelines:
#   1. Keep pattern implementation focused and minimal
#   2. Use dependency injection for testability
#   3. Document pattern usage in component docstrings
#   4. Provide factory methods for common configurations
#   5. Include pattern-specific metrics and logging
#
# Anti-Patterns to Avoid:
#   - Over-abstraction: Don't use pattern where simple code suffices
#   - God object: Keep responsibilities focused
#   - Premature optimization: Profile before optimizing
#   - Tight coupling: Use interfaces, not implementations
#
#
# === Strategy Pattern ===
# Interchangeable algorithm selection
#
#   * Used in: LoadBalancer, RetryPolicy, CacheSystem
#   * Algorithms encapsulated in separate classes
#   * Runtime strategy switching without restart
#   * Strategy validation before activation
#   * Performance benchmarking across strategies
#   * A/B testing support for strategy comparison
#   * Fallback strategy on primary failure
#   * Strategy composition for complex behaviors
#   * Configuration-driven strategy selection
#   * Strategy lifecycle management (init, activate, deactivate)
#   * Metrics per strategy for performance tracking
#   * Hot-swapping with zero-downtime transitions
#
# Implementation Guidelines:
#   1. Keep pattern implementation focused and minimal
#   2. Use dependency injection for testability
#   3. Document pattern usage in component docstrings
#   4. Provide factory methods for common configurations
#   5. Include pattern-specific metrics and logging
#
# Anti-Patterns to Avoid:
#   - Over-abstraction: Don't use pattern where simple code suffices
#   - God object: Keep responsibilities focused
#   - Premature optimization: Profile before optimizing
#   - Tight coupling: Use interfaces, not implementations
#
#
# === Builder Pattern ===
# Step-by-step object construction
#
#   * Used in: EmailBuilder, QueryBuilder, ConfigManager
#   * Fluent interface for readable construction chains
#   * Validation at build time prevents invalid objects
#   * Optional parameters with sensible defaults
#   * Immutable objects produced by builders
#   * Builder reuse for template-based construction
#   * Type-safe builders with generic constraints
#   * Nested builders for complex hierarchies
#   * Builder serialization for deferred construction
#   * Clone and modify support for variations
#   * Builder registry for named configurations
#   * Async builders for remote resource construction
#
# Implementation Guidelines:
#   1. Keep pattern implementation focused and minimal
#   2. Use dependency injection for testability
#   3. Document pattern usage in component docstrings
#   4. Provide factory methods for common configurations
#   5. Include pattern-specific metrics and logging
#
# Anti-Patterns to Avoid:
#   - Over-abstraction: Don't use pattern where simple code suffices
#   - God object: Keep responsibilities focused
#   - Premature optimization: Profile before optimizing
#   - Tight coupling: Use interfaces, not implementations
#
#
# === Command Pattern ===
# Encapsulated operations with undo support
#
#   * Used in: CommandRouter, TaskQueue, ProcessOrchestrator
#   * Commands encapsulate all operation parameters
#   * Undo/redo stack for reversible operations
#   * Command queuing for deferred execution
#   * Batch command execution with transaction semantics
#   * Command logging for audit trail
#   * Priority-based command scheduling
#   * Command serialization for distributed execution
#   * Idempotent commands for safe retries
#   * Command composition for complex operations
#   * Timeout and cancellation support
#   * Command result callbacks and promises
#
# Implementation Guidelines:
#   1. Keep pattern implementation focused and minimal
#   2. Use dependency injection for testability
#   3. Document pattern usage in component docstrings
#   4. Provide factory methods for common configurations
#   5. Include pattern-specific metrics and logging
#
# Anti-Patterns to Avoid:
#   - Over-abstraction: Don't use pattern where simple code suffices
#   - God object: Keep responsibilities focused
#   - Premature optimization: Profile before optimizing
#   - Tight coupling: Use interfaces, not implementations
#
#
# === Repository Pattern ===
# Data access abstraction layer
#
#   * Used in: DocumentStore, KeyValueStore, GraphStore
#   * Abstracts storage mechanism from business logic
#   * Consistent CRUD interface across data types
#   * Query specification objects for complex queries
#   * Unit of Work for transactional consistency
#   * Caching integration at the repository level
#   * Event emission on data changes
#   * Optimistic concurrency with version tracking
#   * Bulk operations for performance
#   * Pagination support with cursor-based iteration
#   * Soft delete with configurable retention
#   * Schema migration support with versioning
#
# Implementation Guidelines:
#   1. Keep pattern implementation focused and minimal
#   2. Use dependency injection for testability
#   3. Document pattern usage in component docstrings
#   4. Provide factory methods for common configurations
#   5. Include pattern-specific metrics and logging
#
# Anti-Patterns to Avoid:
#   - Over-abstraction: Don't use pattern where simple code suffices
#   - God object: Keep responsibilities focused
#   - Premature optimization: Profile before optimizing
#   - Tight coupling: Use interfaces, not implementations
#
#
# === Circuit Breaker Pattern ===
# Fault tolerance for external dependencies
#
#   * Used in: CircuitBreaker, HTTPClient, ServiceDiscovery
#   * Three states: Closed (normal), Open (failing), Half-Open (testing)
#   * Configurable failure threshold and timeout
#   * Sliding window for failure rate calculation
#   * Automatic recovery with gradual traffic increase
#   * Per-operation circuit breakers for fine-grained control
#   * Circuit breaker events for monitoring integration
#   * Manual override for maintenance windows
#   * Fallback execution when circuit is open
#   * Metrics: failure count, state transitions, recovery time
#   * Hierarchical breakers (global, service, operation)
#   * Custom failure detection logic
#
# Implementation Guidelines:
#   1. Keep pattern implementation focused and minimal
#   2. Use dependency injection for testability
#   3. Document pattern usage in component docstrings
#   4. Provide factory methods for common configurations
#   5. Include pattern-specific metrics and logging
#
# Anti-Patterns to Avoid:
#   - Over-abstraction: Don't use pattern where simple code suffices
#   - God object: Keep responsibilities focused
#   - Premature optimization: Profile before optimizing
#   - Tight coupling: Use interfaces, not implementations
#
#
# === Pipeline Pattern ===
# Sequential data processing stages
#
#   * Used in: DataPipeline, TransformPipeline, MLPipeline
#   * Each stage transforms data and passes to the next
#   * Error handling per stage with recovery options
#   * Parallel execution of independent stages
#   * Conditional branching based on intermediate results
#   * Stage metrics and performance monitoring
#   * Backpressure propagation between stages
#   * Stage reuse across different pipelines
#   * Dynamic pipeline construction at runtime
#   * Checkpoint support for long-running pipelines
#   * Fan-out and fan-in for parallel processing
#   * Pipeline visualization and debugging tools
#
# Implementation Guidelines:
#   1. Keep pattern implementation focused and minimal
#   2. Use dependency injection for testability
#   3. Document pattern usage in component docstrings
#   4. Provide factory methods for common configurations
#   5. Include pattern-specific metrics and logging
#
# Anti-Patterns to Avoid:
#   - Over-abstraction: Don't use pattern where simple code suffices
#   - God object: Keep responsibilities focused
#   - Premature optimization: Profile before optimizing
#   - Tight coupling: Use interfaces, not implementations
#
#
# === Proxy Pattern ===
# Controlled access to underlying objects
#
#   * Used in: CacheProxy, ProxyServer, ConnectionPool
#   * Transparent proxying with identical interface
#   * Lazy initialization for expensive resources
#   * Access control and permission checking
#   * Request/response logging and auditing
#   * Connection multiplexing and pooling
#   * Request transformation and enrichment
#   * Response caching with configurable TTL
#   * Load distribution across backend instances
#   * Health monitoring of proxied services
#   * Graceful degradation on backend failure
#   * Protocol translation between client and backend
#
# Implementation Guidelines:
#   1. Keep pattern implementation focused and minimal
#   2. Use dependency injection for testability
#   3. Document pattern usage in component docstrings
#   4. Provide factory methods for common configurations
#   5. Include pattern-specific metrics and logging
#
# Anti-Patterns to Avoid:
#   - Over-abstraction: Don't use pattern where simple code suffices
#   - God object: Keep responsibilities focused
#   - Premature optimization: Profile before optimizing
#   - Tight coupling: Use interfaces, not implementations
#
#
# === Event Sourcing Pattern ===
# State reconstruction from event history
#
#   * Used in: EventSourcer, AuditTrail, EventStream
#   * All state changes stored as immutable events
#   * State reconstructed by replaying event sequence
#   * Temporal queries: state at any point in time
#   * Event schema evolution with upcasting
#   * Snapshot optimization for fast reconstruction
#   * Event deduplication and ordering guarantees
#   * Projection support for materialized views
#   * Event archival with configurable retention
#   * Causation and correlation tracking
#   * Compensating events for corrections
#   * Cross-aggregate event processing
#
# Implementation Guidelines:
#   1. Keep pattern implementation focused and minimal
#   2. Use dependency injection for testability
#   3. Document pattern usage in component docstrings
#   4. Provide factory methods for common configurations
#   5. Include pattern-specific metrics and logging
#
# Anti-Patterns to Avoid:
#   - Over-abstraction: Don't use pattern where simple code suffices
#   - God object: Keep responsibilities focused
#   - Premature optimization: Profile before optimizing
#   - Tight coupling: Use interfaces, not implementations
#
#
# === Saga Pattern ===
# Distributed transaction coordination
#
#   * Used in: SagaOrchestrator, ProcessOrchestrator
#   * Long-running transactions across multiple services
#   * Compensating actions for rollback on failure
#   * Orchestration-based coordination
#   * Choreography-based coordination
#   * Timeout handling with deadline tracking
#   * Retry policies per saga step
#   * State persistence for crash recovery
#   * Concurrent saga execution management
#   * Saga visualization and monitoring
#   * Dead letter handling for failed sagas
#   * Idempotent saga steps for safe retries
#
# Implementation Guidelines:
#   1. Keep pattern implementation focused and minimal
#   2. Use dependency injection for testability
#   3. Document pattern usage in component docstrings
#   4. Provide factory methods for common configurations
#   5. Include pattern-specific metrics and logging
#
# Anti-Patterns to Avoid:
#   - Over-abstraction: Don't use pattern where simple code suffices
#   - God object: Keep responsibilities focused
#   - Premature optimization: Profile before optimizing
#   - Tight coupling: Use interfaces, not implementations
