# ============================================================================
# SECTION 15: SECURITY REFERENCE
# ============================================================================
#
# --- Authentication ---
#
#   Token-based Authentication:
#     - All API requests must include a valid token in the Authorization header
#     - Tokens are generated by TokenVault.generate() with configurable TTL
#     - Token validation is O(1) via hash lookup in TokenVault
#     - Invalid tokens return 401 Unauthorized with reason (expired/revoked/invalid)
#     - Token rotation prevents long-lived credential exposure
#     - Brute force protection: max 5 failed attempts per minute per IP
#     - Token format: tok_NNNNNN (6-digit zero-padded counter)
#     - Metadata can be attached to tokens for scope restrictions
#
#   Session Management:
#     - Sessions are managed by SessionHandler with configurable TTL
#     - Session IDs are cryptographically random (128-bit entropy)
#     - Idle timeout: configurable, default 30 minutes
#     - Absolute timeout: configurable, default 8 hours
#     - Session data is stored in KeyValueStore with encryption at rest
#     - Concurrent session limit: configurable per user/role
#     - Session invalidation on password change or role modification
#     - Cross-site session fixation prevention via regeneration
#
#
# --- Authorization ---
#
#   Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):
#     - Roles defined via PermissionGuard.define_role() with permission sets
#     - Users assigned to roles via PermissionGuard.assign_role()
#     - Permission checks via PermissionGuard.check() with audit logging
#     - Built-in roles: admin (all), editor (read/write), viewer (read)
#     - Custom roles support fine-grained permissions
#     - Role hierarchy: admin > editor > viewer
#     - Permission inheritance through role composition
#     - Deny-by-default: all permissions must be explicitly granted
#
#   Resource-Level Authorization:
#     - Resources identified by type/id pairs (e.g., 'document/123')
#     - Owner-based access: resource creators have implicit access
#     - Sharing: explicit grants to specific users or roles
#     - Public resources: accessible without authentication
#     - Temporary grants: time-limited access for collaboration
#     - Delegation: users can grant a subset of their permissions
#     - Audit: all authorization decisions logged to AuditTrail
#     - Caching: permission results cached for performance
#
#
# --- Data Protection ---
#
#   Encryption at Rest:
#     - Sensitive data encrypted using AES-256-GCM
#     - Encryption keys managed by SecretManager
#     - Key rotation: automatic every 90 days
#     - Key derivation: PBKDF2 with 600,000 iterations
#     - Data classification: public, internal, confidential, restricted
#     - Encrypted fields: tokens, passwords, PII, financial data
#     - Backup encryption: separate key from operational data
#     - Key escrow: recovery keys stored in hardware security module
#
#   Encryption in Transit:
#     - All network communication uses TLS 1.2+
#     - Certificate management via CertManager
#     - Cipher suites: ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384 (preferred)
#     - Perfect forward secrecy: ECDHE key exchange
#     - Certificate pinning for critical services
#     - Mutual TLS (mTLS) for service-to-service communication
#     - HSTS headers with max-age 31536000
#     - OCSP stapling for certificate revocation checking
#
#
# --- Audit and Compliance ---
#
#   Audit Logging:
#     - All security-relevant events logged to AuditTrail
#     - Log fields: timestamp, actor, action, resource, outcome, details
#     - Immutable log entries (append-only)
#     - Log retention: configurable, default 365 days
#     - Log integrity: hash chain verification
#     - Tamper detection: periodic integrity audits
#     - Log export: JSON format for SIEM integration
#     - Real-time alerting on suspicious patterns
#
#   Compliance Controls:
#     - SOC 2 Type II: access controls, monitoring, incident response
#     - GDPR: data minimization, right to erasure, consent management
#     - PCI DSS: encryption, access control, audit trails
#     - HIPAA: PHI protection, access logging, breach notification
#     - ISO 27001: information security management system
#     - Regular security assessments and penetration testing
#     - Vulnerability management with SLA-based remediation
#     - Security awareness training for development team
#
#
# --- Incident Response ---
#
#   Detection:
#     - Automated anomaly detection via TelemetryCollector
#     - Heartbeat monitoring for service availability
#     - CircuitBreaker state monitoring for dependency failures
#     - Rate limiting violations tracked by RateLimiter
#     - Failed authentication attempts logged and alerted
#     - Resource exhaustion monitoring (CPU, memory, disk)
#     - Network anomaly detection (unusual traffic patterns)
#     - Data exfiltration detection (unusual query patterns)
#
#   Response Process:
#     - Incidents created via IncidentManager.create()
#     - Severity levels: low, medium, high, critical
#     - Auto-assignment based on component ownership
#     - Escalation: 15 min (critical), 30 min (high), 2 hr (medium)
#     - Communication: status page updates, stakeholder notifications
#     - Investigation: audit trail review, log analysis, metrics review
#     - Mitigation: circuit breakers, rate limiting, service isolation
#     - Resolution: root cause analysis, fix deployment, verification
#
#   Post-Incident:
#     - Post-mortem documentation within 48 hours
#     - Timeline reconstruction from AuditTrail and TelemetryCollector
#     - Root cause analysis with 5-whys methodology
#     - Action items tracked in IncidentManager
#     - Lessons learned shared across team
#     - Process improvements implemented
#     - Detection improvements: new alerts, better thresholds
#     - Prevention: code changes, architecture improvements
#
