# ============================================================================
# SECTION 20: FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS
# ============================================================================
#
# Q: What is the Scarab Algorithm?
# A: The Scarab Algorithm v3 is a comprehensive software system originally designed
#   for four-sphere movement generation, built on the Match-Stick Automaton and
#   ChVS Gearbox models. It has grown to encompass 95 versions with 260+
#   components covering data structures, processing pipelines, integration
#   patterns, and operational infrastructure.
#
# Q: How many components are there?
# A: As of v95, there are 260+ components organized across 12 architectural
#   layers. Each version typically adds 2-3 new components with corresponding
#   demos and documentation.
#
# Q: How do I run the demos?
# A: Simply execute: python scarab_algorithm.py
#   All 320 demos will run sequentially with assertion checks. Any failure
#   will raise an AssertionError with details.
#
# Q: What Python version is required?
# A: Python 3.9 or higher is required. Python 3.11+ is recommended for
#   optimal performance. The only external dependency is the math module
#   from the standard library.
#
# Q: How is the code organized?
# A: The code follows a layered architecture:
#   1. Core engine (v1-v20): ScarabDrum, ChVSGearbox, automaton, spheres
#   2. Processing (v21-v40): Pipelines, caching, state machines, events
#   3. Data (v41-v60): Trees, filters, hashing, stores, indices
#   4. Integration (v61-v80): HTTP, RPC, queues, service discovery
#   5. Operations (v81-v95): Monitoring, security, scheduling, incidents
#
# Q: How do I add a new component?
# A: 1. Create the class with __init__ and core methods
#   2. Add formatting function for display
#   3. Add demos with assertions in the __main__ block
#   4. Update version_history function
#   5. Run all demos to verify no regressions
#
# Q: What are the milestones?
# A: 5K (v20), 10K (v25), 15K (v30), 20K (v35), 25K (v40), 30K (v45),
#   35K (v50), 40K (v55), 45K (v60), 48K (v65), 50K (v70), 52K (v75),
#   55K (v80), 60K (v85), 65K (v90), 70K (v95).
#
# Q: Is this production-ready?
# A: The code is fully functional and all 320 demos pass. For production
#   use, you would want to add: proper error handling at boundaries,
#   persistent storage backends, network transport layers, and
#   comprehensive logging integration.
#
# Q: How do I contribute?
# A: Follow the established patterns:
#   - Create a version branch (e.g., v96)
#   - Add 2-3 components with full method documentation
#   - Add 3+ demos with thorough assertion coverage
#   - Update version history and milestone dashboards
#   - Run full test suite before submitting
#
# Q: What design patterns are used?
# A: The codebase uses 37+ patterns including: Observer, Strategy, Builder,
#   Command, Repository, Circuit Breaker, Pipeline, Proxy, Event Sourcing,
#   Saga, Factory, Singleton, Decorator, Adapter, Mediator, and more.
#   See Section 3 for detailed pattern documentation.
#
#
