# ============================================================================
# SECTION 24: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORDS
# ============================================================================
#
# ADR-001: Single-file architecture
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Keep all code in a single Python file for simplicity and portability.
# Decision: The entire system is contained in scarab_algorithm.py. This simplifies distribution, ensures atomic versioning, and eliminates import/dependency issues.
# Alternatives: Multi-module package, microservices architecture.
# Consequences: File grows large but remains manageable with clear section markers.
#
# ADR-002: Pure Python implementation
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Use only Python standard library, no external dependencies.
# Decision: All components are implemented in pure Python using only the standard library (math, random, etc.). This ensures maximum portability and eliminates dependency management overhead.
# Alternatives: NumPy for math, Redis for caching, SQLite for storage.
# Consequences: Some operations slower than C-extension equivalents, but sufficient for intended use cases.
#
# ADR-003: Assertion-based testing
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Use built-in assert statements for inline testing.
# Decision: All 320 demos use Python assert statements for validation. This provides immediate feedback on any regression without requiring a test framework.
# Alternatives: pytest, unittest, doctest.
# Consequences: No test isolation, but simple and effective for sequential demo execution.
#
# ADR-004: Class-based component model
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Each component is a self-contained class with clear interfaces.
# Decision: Components follow a consistent pattern: __init__ for setup, public methods for operations, private methods for internals. This provides encapsulation and composability.
# Alternatives: Functional modules, actor model.
# Consequences: Some boilerplate, but clear structure and testability.
#
# ADR-005: Version-based organization
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Organize components by version with clear boundaries.
# Decision: Each version introduces a themed set of components (2-3 per version). Comments mark version boundaries. This provides clear evolution history.
# Alternatives: Feature-based organization, layer-based modules.
# Consequences: Some cross-cutting concerns span versions, resolved by clear dependency documentation.
#
# ADR-006: Milestone-based documentation
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Document milestones at regular line-count intervals.
# Decision: Major milestones (5K, 10K, 15K, ... 70K lines) include dashboard displays showing system statistics. This provides visibility into growth and health.
# Alternatives: Separate documentation files, wiki.
# Consequences: Documentation is always current and versioned with code.
#
# ADR-007: Observer pattern for events
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Use the Observer pattern for inter-component communication.
# Decision: EventBus, PubSubBroker, and EventStream all use publish-subscribe semantics for decoupled communication. This allows components to evolve independently.
# Alternatives: Direct method calls, shared state, message queues.
# Consequences: Some complexity in event routing, but excellent decoupling.
#
# ADR-008: TF-IDF for full-text search
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Use TF-IDF scoring for document relevance ranking.
# Decision: FullTextIndex uses Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency for search result scoring. This provides reasonable relevance without external dependencies.
# Alternatives: BM25, word embeddings, Elasticsearch.
# Consequences: Limited to term-based matching, no semantic understanding.
#
# ADR-009: RBAC for access control
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Use Role-Based Access Control for permission management.
# Decision: PermissionGuard implements RBAC with role definitions, user assignments, and permission checks. This is well-understood and sufficient for most use cases.
# Alternatives: ABAC, ACL, capability-based.
# Consequences: Less flexible than ABAC for complex policies, but simpler to manage.
#
# ADR-010: Token-based authentication
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Use token-based authentication with vault management.
# Decision: TokenVault generates, validates, revokes, and rotates authentication tokens. This is stateless-friendly and supports modern auth patterns.
# Alternatives: Session cookies, JWT, OAuth2.
# Consequences: Requires server-side token storage, but enables fine-grained control.
#
# ADR-011: Comprehensive audit trailing
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Log all security-relevant actions to an immutable audit trail.
# Decision: AuditTrail records who did what to which resource with details. This supports compliance requirements and incident investigation.
# Alternatives: Application logs only, external SIEM.
# Consequences: Storage overhead for high-volume systems, mitigated by max_entries limit.
#
# ADR-012: Heartbeat-based health monitoring
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Use periodic heartbeat signals for service health monitoring.
# Decision: Heartbeat component tracks service health based on periodic beat signals. Services that miss beats beyond a timeout are marked unhealthy.
# Alternatives: Active health checks, passive monitoring.
# Consequences: Requires services to actively send beats, but simple and reliable.
#
# ADR-013: Incident lifecycle management
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Manage incidents through a defined lifecycle with status tracking.
# Decision: IncidentManager tracks incidents from creation through resolution with timeline, assignment, and severity classification.
# Alternatives: External incident management (PagerDuty, OpsGenie).
# Consequences: Basic but functional; can be extended with external integrations.
#
# ADR-014: PageRank for graph ranking
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Use PageRank algorithm for node importance ranking.
# Decision: PageRanker implements the power iteration method with configurable damping factor and iteration count. Used for document and entity ranking.
# Alternatives: HITS, betweenness centrality, eigenvector centrality.
# Consequences: Well-understood algorithm with proven effectiveness.
#
# ADR-015: Fluent builder for email construction
# Status: Accepted
# Context: Use the Builder pattern with fluent interface for email construction.
# Decision: EmailBuilder uses method chaining (set_from().add_to().set_subject().build()) for readable email construction with validation at build time.
# Alternatives: Constructor with many parameters, configuration dict.
# Consequences: Readable and safe construction, prevents invalid emails.
#
