# SECTION 26: OPERATIONAL RUNBOOKS
# ============================================================================
#
# Runbook: Service Restart
# ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
#   Trigger: Service unresponsive (Heartbeat timeout)
#   Steps:
#     1. Verify service status via Heartbeat.get_status(service_id)
#     2. Check TelemetryCollector for recent metrics anomalies
#     3. Review AuditTrail for recent actions on the service
#     4. Check IncidentManager for related open incidents
#     5. If no related incident exists, create one via IncidentManager.create()
#     6. Attempt graceful restart of the service
#     7. Wait for Heartbeat.beat() confirmation
#     8. Verify service health via LoadBalancer health check
#     9. Monitor TelemetryCollector for 15 minutes post-restart
#     10. If stable, resolve the incident. Otherwise, escalate.
#
#   Rollback: N/A (restart is the recovery action)
#   Escalation: After 3 failed restart attempts, page senior engineer
#   Communication: Update status page, notify stakeholders via TemplateMailer
#
# Runbook: Token Rotation
# ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
#   Trigger: Scheduled rotation or security incident
#   Steps:
#     1. Identify tokens due for rotation via TokenVault.get_owner_tokens()
#     2. For each token: new_id = TokenVault.rotate(old_id)
#     3. Verify new token validates: TokenVault.validate(new_id)
#     4. Verify old token is revoked: TokenVault.validate(old_id)
#     5. Log rotation to AuditTrail: audit.log(actor, 'token_rotation', owner)
#     6. Distribute new tokens to affected services
#     7. Monitor for authentication failures post-rotation
#     8. Verify all services using new tokens successfully
#
#   Rollback: Generate new token for affected owner (cannot un-revoke)
#   Escalation: If >5% auth failures post-rotation, page security team
#   Communication: Internal notification via PubSubBroker 'security' topic
#
# Runbook: Capacity Expansion
# ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
#   Trigger: TelemetryCollector shows resource utilization >80%
#   Steps:
#     1. Identify bottleneck via TelemetryCollector.aggregate() metrics
#     2. Review ConnectionPool utilization and resize if needed
#     3. Check CacheSystem hit rate; expand capacity if <90% hit rate
#     4. Review RateLimiter thresholds for potential increase
#     5. Add new backend instances to LoadBalancer
#     6. Update ConsistentHash ring if data layer is bottleneck
#     7. Monitor rebalancing progress
#     8. Verify even distribution via TelemetryCollector per-source metrics
#     9. Update IncidentManager with capacity change details
#     10. Update capacity planning documentation
#
#   Rollback: Remove new instances, revert LoadBalancer config
#   Escalation: If utilization doesn't drop below 80% after expansion
#   Communication: Update infrastructure documentation and runbooks
#
# Runbook: Security Incident Response
# ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
#   Trigger: PermissionGuard detects unauthorized access attempt
#   Steps:
#     1. Create critical incident: IncidentManager.create(title, 'critical')
#     2. Assign to security team: IncidentManager.assign(inc_id, 'security')
#     3. Review AuditTrail for affected user/resource activity
#     4. Revoke compromised tokens: TokenVault.revoke(token_id)
#     5. Update PermissionGuard roles if privilege escalation detected
#     6. Enable enhanced logging via TelemetryCollector
#     7. Review Heartbeat for any service anomalies
#     8. Check EventStream for suspicious event patterns
#     9. Notify affected users via TemplateMailer
#     10. Document findings in incident timeline
#     11. Implement countermeasures (IP block, rate limit adjustment)
#     12. Post-incident review within 48 hours
#
#   Rollback: N/A (response-only procedure)
#   Escalation: Immediate for critical severity; legal team if data breach
#   Communication: Status page, email to affected users, internal briefing
#
# Runbook: Database Connection Pool Exhaustion
# ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
#   Trigger: ConnectionPool acquire timeout errors
#   Steps:
#     1. Check ConnectionPool.get_stats() for pool utilization
#     2. Identify long-running connections via pool metrics
#     3. Check for connection leaks (acquired but not released)
#     4. Review CronScheduler for jobs holding connections
#     5. Temporarily increase pool size: ConnectionPool.resize(new_size)
#     6. Investigate root cause (slow queries, deadlocks)
#     7. Kill long-running connections if necessary
#     8. Monitor pool utilization post-fix
#     9. Update pool size configuration if permanent change needed
#     10. Add alerting threshold for future prevention
#
#   Rollback: Revert pool size to original
#   Escalation: If pool exhaustion persists after increase
#   Communication: Internal alert via PubSubBroker
#
# Runbook: Search Index Rebuild
# ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
#   Trigger: FullTextIndex corruption or schema change
#   Steps:
#     1. Create incident for tracking: IncidentManager.create()
#     2. Backup current index state
#     3. Clear index: remove all documents
#     4. Reload documents from source of truth
#     5. Re-index all documents via FullTextIndex.add_document()
#     6. Verify document count matches expected
#     7. Run validation searches for known queries
#     8. Compare search results with pre-rebuild baseline
#     9. Monitor search latency via TelemetryCollector
#     10. Resolve incident when rebuild verified
#
#   Rollback: Restore from backup
#   Escalation: If rebuild takes >4 hours or results diverge
#   Communication: Degraded search notice on status page
#
# Runbook: Cron Schedule Failure
# ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
#   Trigger: CronScheduler execution log shows errors
#   Steps:
#     1. Check CronScheduler.get_log() for error entries
#     2. Identify failing schedule(s)
#     3. Pause failing schedule: CronScheduler.pause(name)
#     4. Review schedule function for errors
#     5. Check dependencies (external services, data stores)
#     6. Fix root cause
#     7. Resume schedule: CronScheduler.resume(name)
#     8. Monitor next few executions for success
#     9. Log resolution to AuditTrail
#     10. Add error handling improvements if needed
#
#   Rollback: Keep schedule paused until fix verified
#   Escalation: If schedule is business-critical and down >1 hour
#   Communication: Team notification via PubSubBroker
#
# Runbook: Email Delivery Failure
# ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
#   Trigger: TemplateMailer.send() returns False or errors
#   Steps:
#     1. Verify template exists: TemplateMailer.list_templates()
#     2. Check template rendering: TemplateMailer.preview(name, vars)
#     3. Validate email addresses using EmailBuilder.validate()
#     4. Check for rate limiting on email provider
#     5. Review recent email delivery log
#     6. Test with simple email via EmailBuilder
#     7. If provider issue, switch to backup provider
#     8. Retry failed emails with JobRunner (max_retries=3)
#     9. Monitor delivery success rate
#     10. Log resolution and update IncidentManager
#
#   Rollback: Queue emails for later retry
#   Escalation: If delivery failure rate >10% for >30 minutes
#   Communication: Internal team notification, defer non-critical emails
#
# Runbook: Permission Policy Update
# ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
#   Trigger: Role or permission changes required
#   Steps:
#     1. Document current state: PermissionGuard.get_all_roles()
#     2. Plan changes with stakeholder approval
#     3. Create audit entry: AuditTrail.log(admin, 'policy_change', details)
#     4. Apply role changes: PermissionGuard.define_role()
#     5. Update user assignments: PermissionGuard.assign_role()
#     6. Verify changes with test checks: PermissionGuard.check()
#     7. Notify affected users via TemplateMailer
#     8. Monitor PermissionGuard.get_check_log() for anomalies
#     9. Review access patterns for 24 hours post-change
#     10. Document final state and close change request
#
#   Rollback: Revert to previous role definitions
#   Escalation: If users report access issues post-change
#   Communication: Email to affected users, internal announcement
#
# Runbook: Telemetry Data Gap
# ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
#   Trigger: TelemetryCollector shows missing data from source
#   Steps:
#     1. Identify missing source: compare get_sources() vs expected
#     2. Check Heartbeat status for source service
#     3. If service unhealthy, follow Service Restart runbook
#     4. If service healthy, check collection endpoint
#     5. Verify TelemetryCollector configuration for source
#     6. Check network connectivity to source
#     7. Review source logs for telemetry emission errors
#     8. Restart telemetry agent on source if needed
#     9. Verify data flow resumes: TelemetryCollector.collect() succeeds
#     10. Backfill missing data if possible
#
#   Rollback: N/A (data gaps cannot be undone, only backfilled)
#   Escalation: If gap >1 hour for critical source
#   Communication: Update monitoring dashboard annotations
#
